/-
# Hypothesis testing and the Neyman-Pearson power bound

This file formalises the mathematical core of Section 2 of the original
analysis.

Section 2 of the text proposes deciding whether a passage is AI-generated by
**watermarking plus a z-test**, and claims that without a protocol and a
watermark the provenance of plain text cannot be trusted.

The text **gives no** theoretical upper bound for that test. This file supplies
one. For every statistic-based test (a z-test, a t-test, any detector, any
threshold), the **discriminating power cannot exceed** the total variation
distance `TV(p, q)`. This is not a defect of some particular test. It is the
**common ceiling of all tests**.

## Main results

* `totalVariation_eq_max_sum` — `TV = Σᵢ max(pᵢ − qᵢ, 0)`, the sum over the
  positive part.
* `mass_diff_eq_sum` — `mass p S − mass q S = Σ_{i∈S}(pᵢ − qᵢ)`.
* `mass_sub_le_tv` — **upper bound**: the probability difference on any set is
  at most `TV`.
* `mass_posSet_eq_tv` — **the bound is attained**: equality holds at
  `S = posSet p q = {i | pᵢ ≥ qᵢ}`.
* `power_le_tv` — **Neyman-Pearson power bound**: for any test `S`,
  `power ≤ FPR + TV`.
* `abs_mass_diff_le_tv` — **two-sided version**: `|power − FPR| ≤ TV`.
* `abs_mass_diff_attains` — that two-sided bound is attained too, so `TV` is
  exactly the advantage of the optimal test.
* `tv_eq_zero_iff_mass_diff` — `TV = 0` ⟺ the probability difference is
  exactly 0 on every set.
* `no_threshold_escapes_tv` — no threshold test escapes this bound.

## One correction: the manuscript's `confusable_le_tv` is false

An early draft wrote "`mass p S + mass q S ≤ 1 + TV`". This is **wrong**: take
`p = q` and `S = univ`. The left side is `= 2`, the right side is
`= 1 + TV(p,p) = 1`.

The identity in the confusion matrix is `TP + FN = 1` (not `TP + FP ≤ 1`), so
the meaningful two-sided criterion is

    |TP − FP| = |Youden's J| ≤ TV

which is `abs_mass_diff_le_tv` in this file. Formalisation does here exactly
what it is supposed to do: it **kills the claim outright**.

## Why this supports Section 2 directly

Let the watermark detector's statistic `T` be distributed as `p` on human text
and as `q` on AI text, and take any threshold `τ` to get the discriminating set
`S_τ = {x : T(x) > τ}`. Then `mass_sub_le_tv` gives

    power(S_τ) − FPR(S_τ) ≤ TV(p, q)

The conclusion of Section 2 is therefore not merely "one should use a z-test".
It is the stronger statement that **when `TV` itself is small, no statistical
test rescues the observation-layer determination**. Going past this bound
requires the protocol layer (signatures, watermark lineage), not a better
statistic. This is the quantitative basis for judgement 2 in Section 5 of the
text.

-/

import Mathlib.Tactic
import Mathlib.Data.Finset.Basic
import Provenance.Basic

open Finset
open scoped BigOperators

namespace Provenance

variable {n : ℕ}

/-- The mass of the distribution `p` on the set `S`. -/
def mass (p : PMF n) (S : Finset (Fin n)) : ℝ := ∑ i ∈ S, p.val i

theorem mass_nonneg (p : PMF n) (S : Finset (Fin n)) : 0 ≤ mass p S := by
  unfold mass
  exact Finset.sum_nonneg fun i _ => p.nonneg i

theorem mass_empty (p : PMF n) : mass p ∅ = 0 := by
  unfold mass; simp

theorem mass_univ (p : PMF n) : mass p (Finset.univ) = 1 := by
  show (∑ i : Fin n, p.val i) = 1
  exact p.sum_one

/-- Mass is additive: the mass on `S` plus the mass on its complement is 1. -/
theorem mass_add_compl (p : PMF n) (S : Finset (Fin n)) :
    mass p S + mass p (Finset.univ \ S) = 1 := by
  have key : (∑ i : Fin n, p.val i)
      = ∑ i ∈ Finset.univ \ S, p.val i + ∑ i ∈ S, p.val i :=
    (Finset.sum_sdiff (Finset.subset_univ S)).symm
  have huniv : (∑ i : Fin n, p.val i) = 1 := p.sum_one
  unfold mass
  linarith

theorem mass_le_one (p : PMF n) (S : Finset (Fin n)) : mass p S ≤ 1 := by
  have h0 := mass_nonneg p (Finset.univ \ S)
  have h1 := mass_add_compl p S
  linarith

/-- Mass does not decrease as the set grows. -/
theorem mass_mono (p : PMF n) {S T : Finset (Fin n)} (h : S ⊆ T) :
    mass p S ≤ mass p T := by
  unfold mass
  exact Finset.sum_le_sum_of_subset_of_nonneg h fun i _ _ => p.nonneg i

theorem mass_union (p : PMF n) (S T : Finset (Fin n)) :
    mass p (S ∪ T) = mass p S + mass p (T \ S) := by
  have hsd : (S ∪ T) \ S = T \ S := by
    ext i
    rw [Finset.mem_sdiff, Finset.mem_sdiff, Finset.mem_union]
    constructor
    · rintro ⟨h, hn⟩
      exact ⟨h.resolve_left hn, hn⟩
    · rintro ⟨h, hn⟩
      exact ⟨Or.inr h, hn⟩
  unfold mass
  calc (∑ i ∈ S ∪ T, p.val i)
      = (∑ i ∈ (S ∪ T) \ S, p.val i) + ∑ i ∈ S, p.val i := by
        symm
        exact Finset.sum_sdiff fun i hi => Finset.mem_union.mpr (Or.inl hi)
    _ = ∑ i ∈ T \ S, p.val i + ∑ i ∈ S, p.val i := by rw [hsd]
    _ = (∑ i ∈ S, p.val i) + ∑ i ∈ T \ S, p.val i := add_comm _ _

/-! ## 1. `TV` characterised through sets -/

/-- `max(x, 0) = (x + |x|)/2`. -/
theorem max_eq_half (x : ℝ) : max x 0 = (x + |x|) / 2 := by
  by_cases h : 0 ≤ x
  · rw [max_eq_left h, abs_of_nonneg h]; ring
  · have hx : x < 0 := lt_of_not_ge h
    rw [max_eq_right (le_of_lt hx), abs_of_neg hx]; ring

/-- `TV(p,q) = Σᵢ max(pᵢ − qᵢ, 0)`: the sum over the positive part.

Since `Σᵢ(pᵢ − qᵢ) = 0`, it follows that
`Σᵢ max(pᵢ − qᵢ, 0) = ½Σᵢ|pᵢ − qᵢ| = TV`. -/
theorem totalVariation_eq_max_sum (p q : PMF n) :
    totalVariation p q = ∑ i : Fin n, max (p.val i - q.val i) 0 := by
  unfold totalVariation
  have hz : ∑ i : Fin n, (p.val i - q.val i) = 0 := by
    rw [Finset.sum_sub_distrib, p.sum_one, q.sum_one]
    norm_num
  have hstep : ∀ i : Fin n, max (p.val i - q.val i) 0
      = (1 / 2 : ℝ) * (p.val i - q.val i) + (1 / 2 : ℝ) * |p.val i - q.val i| := by
    intro i
    rw [max_eq_half]
    ring
  have hmain : ∑ i : Fin n, max (p.val i - q.val i) 0
      = ∑ i : Fin n, (1 / 2 : ℝ) * (p.val i - q.val i)
        + ∑ i : Fin n, (1 / 2 : ℝ) * |p.val i - q.val i| := by
    rw [Finset.sum_congr rfl (fun i _ => hstep i), Finset.sum_add_distrib]
  rw [hmain, ← Finset.mul_sum, ← Finset.mul_sum, hz, mul_zero, zero_add]

/-- The discriminating set: the part where the difference is positive, that is
the discriminating set of the NP test.

`≥` on `ℝ` has no executable decision procedure, hence the `noncomputable`
tag. -/
noncomputable def posSet (p q : PMF n) : Finset (Fin n) :=
  Finset.univ.filter (fun i => p.val i - q.val i ≥ 0)

/-! ## 2. The total variation theorem in set form -/

/-- The **probability difference** of two distributions on a set equals the sum
of the differences. -/
theorem mass_diff_eq_sum (p q : PMF n) (S : Finset (Fin n)) :
    mass p S - mass q S = ∑ i ∈ S, (p.val i - q.val i) := by
  unfold mass
  rw [Finset.sum_sub_distrib]

/-- **Upper bound**: the probability difference on any set is at most `TV`.

This is the foundation of the whole file: **for every test, "hits minus false
alarms" stays at or below `TV`.** -/
theorem mass_sub_le_tv (p q : PMF n) (S : Finset (Fin n)) :
    mass p S - mass q S ≤ totalVariation p q := by
  have key : (∑ i ∈ S, (p.val i - q.val i))
      ≤ ∑ i : Fin n, max (p.val i - q.val i) 0 := by
    calc (∑ i ∈ S, (p.val i - q.val i))
        = ∑ i : Fin n, if i ∈ S then p.val i - q.val i else 0 := by
          rw [← Finset.sum_filter, Finset.filter_mem_eq_inter,
            Finset.inter_comm, Finset.inter_univ]
      _ ≤ ∑ i : Fin n, max (p.val i - q.val i) 0 := by
            refine Finset.sum_le_sum fun i _ => ?_
            by_cases hi : i ∈ S
            · rw [if_pos hi]
              exact le_max_left _ _
            · rw [if_neg hi]
              exact le_max_right _ _
  rw [mass_diff_eq_sum, totalVariation_eq_max_sum]
  exact key

/-- **The bound is attained**: at `posSet`, namely `{i | pᵢ ≥ qᵢ}`, the
probability difference is exactly `TV`.

This is the discrete form of the Neyman-Pearson lemma. -/
theorem mass_posSet_eq_tv (p q : PMF n) :
    mass p (posSet p q) - mass q (posSet p q) = totalVariation p q := by
  rw [mass_diff_eq_sum, posSet, Finset.sum_filter]
  have key : (∑ i : Fin n, if p.val i - q.val i ≥ 0 then p.val i - q.val i else 0)
      = ∑ i : Fin n, max (p.val i - q.val i) 0 := by
    refine Finset.sum_congr rfl fun i _ => ?_
    by_cases h : p.val i - q.val i ≥ 0
    · rw [if_pos h, max_eq_left h]
    · rw [if_neg h, max_eq_right (le_of_not_ge h)]
  rw [key, ← totalVariation_eq_max_sum]

/-! ## 3. Neyman-Pearson: an upper bound on the discriminating power of any
test

Conventions:
* the test `S` is the set of observations judged to be human;
* **power** `power = mass p S`: the fraction of human data correctly identified;
* **false positive rate** `FPR = mass q S`: the fraction of AI data wrongly
  judged to be human.

-/

/-- **The Neyman-Pearson power bound**: every test satisfies
`power ≤ FPR + TV`.

This is a universal ceiling over **all** statistical tests, z-tests included,
and it does not depend on the form of the test. -/
theorem power_le_tv (p q : PMF n) (S : Finset (Fin n)) :
    mass p S ≤ mass q S + totalVariation p q := by
  have h := mass_sub_le_tv p q S
  linarith

/-- The bound is attained: the optimal test, the NP test, takes `posSet`. -/
theorem power_attains_tv (p q : PMF n) :
    mass p (posSet p q) = mass q (posSet p q) + totalVariation p q := by
  have h := mass_posSet_eq_tv p q
  linarith

/-- **The two-sided bound**: `|power − FPR| ≤ TV`.

Applying `mass_sub_le_tv` to the complement gives the reverse inequality, and
merging the two gives a **two-sided** restriction inside `TV`. That is the
upper bound on Youden's J. -/
theorem abs_mass_diff_le_tv (p q : PMF n) (S : Finset (Fin n)) :
    |mass p S - mass q S| ≤ totalVariation p q := by
  have h1 := mass_sub_le_tv p q S
  have hc := mass_sub_le_tv p q (Finset.univ \ S)
  have hp := mass_add_compl p S
  have hq := mass_add_compl q S
  rw [abs_le]
  constructor <;> linarith

/-- **The two-sided bound is attained as well**, so `TV` is the **exact**
discriminating advantage of the optimal test.

Together with `Provenance.accuracy_bounds` (an accuracy advantage of `TV/2`)
and this theorem (a discriminating difference of `TV`), the characterisation of
identifiability at layer 1 is now complete. -/
theorem abs_mass_diff_attains (p q : PMF n) :
    |mass p (posSet p q) - mass q (posSet p q)| = totalVariation p q := by
  rw [mass_posSet_eq_tv, abs_of_nonneg (totalVariation_nonneg p q)]

/-- **No signal ⇒ no advantage**: when `TV = 0` the probability difference is
exactly 0 on every set. -/
theorem tv_eq_zero_mass_diff (p q : PMF n) (S : Finset (Fin n))
    (h : totalVariation p q = 0) : mass p S = mass q S := by
  have h1 := mass_sub_le_tv p q S
  have h2 := abs_mass_diff_le_tv p q S
  rw [h] at h1 h2
  have habs : |mass p S - mass q S| = 0 :=
    le_antisymm h2 (abs_nonneg (mass p S - mass q S))
  exact sub_eq_zero.mp (abs_eq_zero.mp habs)

/-- **The converse has to restrict the discriminating set**: `TV = 0` is worth
discussing only when the difference vanishes on the **optimal**
discriminating set.

(An early draft wrote this as `TV = 0` ↔ `mass p S = mass q S` for an
arbitrary `S`. That is false: with `S = ∅` both sides hold whatever `TV` is.
Formalisation kills it outright once again.) -/
theorem mass_diff_zero_iff_tv (p q : PMF n) :
    mass p (posSet p q) - mass q (posSet p q) = 0 ↔ totalVariation p q = 0 := by
  constructor
  · intro h
    rw [mass_posSet_eq_tv] at h
    exact h
  · intro h
    rw [mass_posSet_eq_tv, h]

/-- The full version: `TV = 0` ⟺ there is no probability difference on **every**
set.

This is the accurate formalisation of "the two distributions are
indistinguishable". Note that it has to quantify over all `S`: a single `S` does
not suffice, since `S = ∅` always gives 0. -/
theorem tv_eq_zero_iff_all_masses (p q : PMF n) :
    totalVariation p q = 0 ↔ ∀ S : Finset (Fin n), mass p S = mass q S := by
  constructor
  · intro h S
    exact tv_eq_zero_mass_diff p q S h
  · intro h
    have h' := h (posSet p q)
    have hz : mass p (posSet p q) - mass q (posSet p q) = 0 := by
      rw [h']
      ring
    exact (mass_diff_zero_iff_tv p q).mp hz

/-! ## 4. Interface with Section 2 of the text: the power ceiling of the z-test -/

/-- **No z-test, and no other threshold test, can break the TV ceiling.**

Let the watermark statistic `T` be distributed as `p` on human text and as `q`
on AI text, and take any threshold `τ` to get the discriminating set
`S_τ = {x : T(x) > τ}`. Then for **every** `τ`

    power(S_τ) − FPR(S_τ) ≤ TV(p, q)

So **a finer threshold, or a better tuned statistic, breaks TV no better than
anything else**. The conclusion of Section 2 should be strengthened
accordingly: when the observation-layer determination fails, the bottleneck is
that `TV` itself is too small, not that the test is not good enough. -/
theorem no_threshold_escapes_tv (p q : PMF n) (S : Finset (Fin n)) :
    mass p S - mass q S ≤ totalVariation p q :=
  mass_sub_le_tv p q S

/-- The **largest** advantage of any test is exactly `TV`: reached by `posSet`,
and exceeded by nothing. -/
theorem optimal_test_advantage (p q : PMF n) :
    mass p (posSet p q) - mass q (posSet p q) = totalVariation p q :=
  mass_posSet_eq_tv p q

end Provenance